/**
 * \page guide_nodelist Node-list containers
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * Linked lists whose nodes never move, so references, pointers, and iterators stay
 * valid across edits and splicing a node between lists is O(1). The family splits by
 * where the nodes live: heap-allocating (\ref worse::core::container::List "List" /
 * \ref worse::core::container::ForwardList "ForwardList"), zero-heap inline node pools
 * with a hard cap (\ref worse::core::container::FixedList "FixedList" /
 * \ref worse::core::container::FixedSList "FixedSList"), and allocation-free
 * caller-owned nodes (\ref worse::core::container::IntrusiveList "IntrusiveList"). All
 * are EASTL/Unreal-flavored, deliberately NOT std clones: cached O(1) `size()` and a
 * full allocation-free algorithm surface (R25–R31).
 *
 * \section gn_list List<T>
 * Allocating doubly-linked list with an embedded circular sentinel: the general-purpose
 * pointer-stable list with `back()`. (R25, R29, R30, R43)
 * - **Memory:** one heap node per element (a `T` plus two link pointers); the default
 *   allocator force-inlines to bare `new`/`delete` (R43). Cache-hostile (a pointer chase
 *   per element); supply a pool/arena allocator for hot paths.
 * - **Complexity:** `pushFront`/`pushBack`/`popFront`/`popBack`/`insert`/`erase` and
 *   single-element/whole-list `splice` are O(1); cross-list range `splice` is
 *   O(distance) to keep the cached `size()` correct (R26); `merge`/`sort` are
 *   O(n log n) STABLE (allocation-free in-place binned merge sort); `reverse` is O(n);
 *   `size()` is cached O(1) (R30).
 * - **Iterators / stability:** bidirectional; reverse iterators provided. Iterators,
 *   references, and pointers stay STABLE across every edit except erasing the element
 *   they name; `splice` keeps moved-node iterators valid; `sort`/`merge`/`reverse`
 *   invalidate nothing. NOT trivially relocatable — the self-referential sentinel breaks
 *   under memcpy (R29).
 * - **Use when:** you need a stable-reference sequence with O(1) splice between lists and
 *   access at both ends (LRU chains, command/undo queues, free lists).
 * - **Avoid when:** the access pattern is a contiguous scan or random index — prefer
 *   `Array`; or when the element count is bounded and per-frame — prefer `FixedList`.
 * - **Notes:** `~std::list` (EASTL shape). The default-allocator force-inline ties/beats
 *   `std::list` on push (R43).
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using worse::core::container::List;
 *
 * List<int> a{3, 1, 2};
 * a.sort();                 // stable O(n log n): {1, 2, 3}
 *
 * List<int> b{4, 5};
 * a.splice(a.end(), b);     // O(1): steal all of b; a == {1,2,3,4,5}, b empty
 * int& last = a.back();     // O(1) tail access
 * \endcode
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::List
 *
 * \section gn_forward_list ForwardList<T>
 * Allocating singly-linked list (EASTL slist shape) with the `*After` mutation API and a
 * smaller one-pointer node. (R25, R30, R43)
 * - **Memory:** one heap node per element (a `T` plus a single forward pointer) — smaller
 *   and lighter than `List`; null-terminated, not circular. Default allocator force-inlined
 *   to bare `new`/`delete` (R43). Cache-hostile, same as `List`.
 * - **Complexity:** `pushFront`/`emplaceFront`/`insertAfter`/`eraseAfter` and
 *   single-element `spliceAfter` are O(1); whole-list `spliceAfter` and `resize` are
 *   O(n) (no tail pointer); cross-list range `spliceAfter` is O(distance) (R26);
 *   `merge`/`sort` O(n log n) STABLE; `reverse` O(n); `size()` cached O(1) (R30).
 * - **Iterators / stability:** forward only — no `operator--`, no reverse iterators, no
 *   `back()`/`pushBack()`/tail. Mutate "after" a position you hold; `beforeBegin()` is the
 *   handle to the head. Stability identical to `List` (insert never invalidates; erase only
 *   the erased element; relinking algorithms invalidate nothing). NOT trivially relocatable
 *   (R29).
 * - **Use when:** front-only churn or hash-chain buckets / simple stacks / free lists where
 *   the smaller node matters and you never need the tail.
 * - **Avoid when:** you need `back()`, backward iteration, or O(1) whole-list splice — use
 *   `List`; or bounded per-frame storage — use `FixedSList`.
 * - **Notes:** `~std::forward_list`. The force-inlined allocation makes `ForwardList` push
 *   beat both `std` and EASTL (R43).
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using worse::core::container::ForwardList;
 *
 * ForwardList<int> fl{2, 3};
 * fl.pushFront(1);                         // O(1): {1, 2, 3}
 * auto it = fl.insertAfter(fl.begin(), 9); // splice 9 after the head element: {1, 9, 2, 3}
 * fl.eraseAfter(fl.beforeBegin());         // drop the first element: {9, 2, 3}
 * \endcode
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::ForwardList
 *
 * \section gn_fixed_list FixedList<T, N>
 * Fixed-capacity doubly-linked list over an inline, zero-heap node pool with a hard cap
 * (EASTL `fixed_list` analogue). (R6, R46)
 * - **Memory:** up to `N` nodes live in the object's own aligned byte buffer, handed out by
 *   a free-list — ZERO heap traffic, no per-node cache-line indirection.
 * - **Complexity:** `pushFront`/`pushBack`/`insert`/`erase` O(1); in-place
 *   `sort`/`merge`/`remove`/`removeIf`/`unique`/`reverse` are full-power and
 *   allocation-free (`merge` is element-wise into this pool, O(n), capacity-checked). No
 *   `splice` (node identity cannot cross two distinct inline pools); `size()` O(1).
 * - **Iterators / stability:** bidirectional; reverse iterators provided. Stability matches
 *   `List` (insert never invalidates; erase only the erased element) EXCEPT that moving or
 *   swapping the container relocates the inline nodes and DOES invalidate iterators/pointers.
 * - **Use when:** bounded, per-frame, zero-allocation doubly-linked storage with stable
 *   references and two-ended access.
 * - **Avoid when:** the count is unbounded (it fails closed at `N`) or you need O(1) splice
 *   between lists — use `List`.
 * - **Notes:** overflow past `N` ABORTS in every build (`WE_VERIFY`, R6) — use
 *   `tryEmplaceFront`/`tryEmplaceBack` (returning `nullptr` on a full pool) for the
 *   non-aborting path; `remaining()`/`full()` pre-check capacity (R46).
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using worse::core::container::FixedList;
 *
 * FixedList<int, 4> fl;
 * fl.pushBack(1);
 * fl.pushBack(2);
 * if (int* p = fl.tryEmplaceBack(3)) // non-aborting guard: nullptr when full
 * {
 *     *p += 10;                      // 13
 * }
 * // fl.pushBack(...) past N would abort (R6); check fl.remaining() first.
 * \endcode
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::FixedList
 *
 * \section gn_fixed_slist FixedSList<T, N>
 * Fixed-capacity singly-linked list over an inline, zero-heap node pool with a hard cap
 * (EASTL `fixed_slist` analogue) — the smallest, most cache-friendly node list. (R6, R46)
 * - **Memory:** up to `N` one-pointer nodes in the object's own aligned buffer, handed out by
 *   a free-list — ZERO heap traffic, smallest node of the family.
 * - **Complexity:** `emplaceFront`/`insertAfter`/`eraseAfter` O(1); in-place
 *   `sort`/`merge`/`remove`/`removeIf`/`unique`/`reverse` allocation-free (`merge`
 *   element-wise into this pool, O(n), capacity-checked); `resize` O(n); `size()` O(1).
 * - **Iterators / stability:** forward only (same `*After` API as `ForwardList`,
 *   `beforeBegin()` is the head handle). Stability matches `ForwardList` EXCEPT
 *   move/swap relocate the inline nodes and invalidate iterators/pointers. No `spliceAfter`
 *   (node identity cannot cross pools).
 * - **Use when:** bounded, per-frame, zero-allocation front-only/`*After` storage where the
 *   one-pointer node is the tightest fit.
 * - **Avoid when:** unbounded count, or you need backward iteration / O(1) splice between
 *   lists.
 * - **Notes:** overflow past `N` ABORTS in every build (`WE_VERIFY`, R6) — use
 *   `tryEmplaceFront`/`tryEmplaceAfter` for the non-aborting path; `remaining()`/`full()`
 *   pre-check capacity (R46).
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using worse::core::container::FixedSList;
 *
 * FixedSList<int, 4> sl;
 * auto it = sl.insertAfter(sl.beforeBegin(), 1); // O(1): {1}
 * sl.insertAfter(it, 2);                         // {1, 2}
 * if (int* p = sl.tryEmplaceAfter(it, 9))        // non-aborting guard: nullptr when full
 * {
 *     (void)p;                                   // {1, 9, 2}
 * }
 * \endcode
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::FixedSList
 *
 * \section gn_intrusive_list IntrusiveList<T>
 * Embedded-node circular doubly-linked list with NO allocation: the element embeds the list
 * hook and the caller owns the node storage (boost::intrusive::list analogue). (R45)
 * - **Memory:** allocates and owns NOTHING — the link pair lives inside `T` (which derives
 *   from `IntrusiveListNode`). Link/unlink is pure O(1) pointer surgery with zero heap
 *   traffic. The list never constructs or destroys elements (lifetime is the caller's).
 * - **Complexity:** `pushBack`/`pushFront`/`insert`/`erase`/`remove`/`popBack`/`popFront`
 *   are O(1); `iteratorTo(value)` is an O(1) handle (no search). `size()` cached O(1) — so
 *   all mutation MUST go through the list API.
 * - **Iterators / stability:** bidirectional; reverse iterators provided. Stable — nodes
 *   never move. Move-only (copying a linked element would copy stale links). A node may be in
 *   at most one `IntrusiveList<T>` at a time. NOT trivially relocatable (the sentinel reseats
 *   on move/swap).
 * - **Use when:** objects already live elsewhere and must thread onto a list without a
 *   per-node allocation (active entities, dirty-flag chains, free lists), or an object needs
 *   membership in multiple lists (one hook per list).
 * - **Avoid when:** you do not control the element type (cannot add the hook) or want the
 *   container to own element lifetime — use `List`.
 * - **Notes:** `erase` asserts `pos != end()` — erasing the anchor would silently corrupt
 *   `size()` and the ring (R45). `unlink`/`remove`/`clear` null the detached node's links.
 * \code{.cpp}
 * using worse::core::container::IntrusiveList;
 * using worse::core::container::IntrusiveListNode;
 *
 * struct Entity : IntrusiveListNode // embed the hook
 * {
 *     int id;
 * };
 *
 * Entity e0{.id = 0};
 * Entity e1{.id = 1};
 *
 * IntrusiveList<Entity> active;
 * active.pushBack(e0);                       // O(1), no allocation
 * active.pushBack(e1);
 * active.erase(active.iteratorTo(e0));       // O(1) unlink; e0 is left intact, caller owns it
 * \endcode
 * Full API: \ref worse::core::container::IntrusiveList
 *
 * \section gn_pick When to pick which
 * - **List** — the general stable-reference list: O(1) splice between lists and `back()`.
 * - **ForwardList** — lighter (one-pointer node), front-only churn, no tail/`back()`.
 * - **FixedList / FixedSList** — bounded and zero-heap (per-frame work); doubly- vs
 *   singly-linked; both fail closed at `N` with `try*` escape hatches.
 * - **IntrusiveList** — objects that already live elsewhere, need membership in multiple
 *   lists, or demand zero allocation; the caller owns node storage and element lifetime.
 */
